After Creating All Files

Check structure:

Shell
find docs -type f

Expected:

Plain Text
docs/linux/linux-basic.txt
docs/linux/file-management.txt
docs/linux/process-management.txt
 
docs/security/password-policy.txt
docs/security/firewall-basics.txt
 
docs/network/network-basics.txt
docs/network/troubleshooting.txt
 
docs/runbooks/db-recovery.txt
docs/runbooks/server-restart.txt
 
docs/incidents/cpu-high.txt
docs/incidents/database-down.txt
Why Are We Creating These Files?

Later, when a user asks:

Plain Text
How do I restart a server?

AI will read:

Plain Text
docs/runbooks/server-restart.txt

When user asks:

Plain Text
What is DNS?

AI will read:

Plain Text
docs/network/network-basics.txt

When user asks:

Plain Text
What is password policy?

AI will read:

Plain Text
docs/security/password-policy.txt
 

So these files become the knowledge books for the AI. The more documents you add, the smarter your RAG system becomes.

Minimum for Practice

Create at least:

Plain Text
3 Linux files
2 Security files
2 Network files
2 Runbook files
2 Incident files

Total:

Plain Text
11 documents

This is enough to start the RAG implementation in the next phase.

